course: dictionary
lesson: 10460
title: Tsar: one word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists them all, and the sentence tells you which one fits. «In 1654 at Pereiaslav, Khmelnytsky agreed to a military alliance with the Tsar of Muscovy.» Here a tsar is a ruler, like an emperor, because a ruler is the person who leads a country.
explain: Tsar is the most common spelling in English, but czar and tzar mean exactly the same thing. They are spellings of one word, not three different words.
explain: The second meaning is informal. In the news a government may name a «drugs tsar», meaning a person given strong power over one area. Ask yourself: who has power, and over what? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: People can read the same event in different ways. The text says the Cossacks saw the deal as an alliance, but later Moscow treated it as Ukraine joining Russia. When a word is part of a historical story, check the context before you decide what it means.
fact: A tsar was an emperor of Russia before 1917.
fact: Tsar, czar and tzar are all spellings of the same word.
fact: The wife of a tsar was called the tsarina.
fact: Informally, a tsar is a person a government gives strong power over one area, such as energy or schools.
fact: Nicholas II was the last tsar, and he lost power in the Russian Revolution of 1917.
exercise: «In 1654 at Pereiaslav, Khmelnytsky agreed to a military alliance with the Tsar of Muscovy.» What is a tsar in this sentence? | A type of military boat | A kind of winter coat | *A ruler, like an emperor | A large Russian meal
exercise: The government has appointed a drugs tsar. What does tsar mean here? | A very old song | *A person given strong power over one area | A king's pet dog | A ship's captain
exercise: Which of these is another spelling of tsar? | star | *czar | tsunami
exercise: What was the wife of a tsar called? | Star | Tzarette | *Tsarina | Tsunami
test: Which tsar lost power in the Russian Revolution of 1917? | Ivan the Terrible | Peter the Great | *Nicholas II
test: In the sentence «The Cossacks saw it as an alliance», what does it show? | *Different people can read the same event in different ways | The Cossacks were all tsars | Russia had no army | The alliance was a secret
exam: The news says «the government has named a new schools tsar». What does tsar most likely mean? | An emperor of Russia before 1917 | A school holiday | *Someone given strong power over schools | A school bus driver
